WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:01.812
Why treat detections as code?

2
00:00:01.812 --> 00:00:02.850
Two reasons.

3
00:00:02.850 --> 00:00:05.763
A rule that can't be shown
to fire isn't a control.

4
00:00:05.763 --> 00:00:08.285
And a rule that nobody can trace back to a

5
00:00:08.285 --> 00:00:11.113
reviewed file isn't a
change anyone approved.

6
00:00:11.113 --> 00:00:14.982
By the end of this chapter you can
keep custom detections as files,

7
00:00:14.982 --> 00:00:19.066
prove each one fires on its known-bad
event and stays quiet otherwise,

8
00:00:19.066 --> 00:00:22.863
and produce a deployment plan that
an approver signs by its hash.

9
00:00:22.863 --> 00:00:27.001
It's for the developers and the
detection engineer who owns this gate.

10
00:00:27.001 --> 00:00:31.948
The rule lifecycle itself, author,
test, alert-only, promote and review,

11
00:00:31.948 --> 00:00:37.920
is the Security Operations book's; this
chapter builds its test and its deployment.
